 and  interests that said it is an intriguing  topic of discussion as characters with a  lot of thought put into them can't  convey a host of information to the  viewer at a glance for instance if you  look at these individual parts you  already know what they are that's an  engine those are wheels etc etc combine  these parrots with a host of others and  it becomes what everyone recognizes as a  car but if you take the wheels off of  this car people maybe even you would  still refer to this as a car what if you  remove the engine the brakes when does  this car stop being a car because an  engine isn't a car it's an engine so  there must be a point this car stops  being a car this is what I mean by bad  character design being a nebulous  subject when does a bad design become a  good design or vice versa a youtuber by  the name of super eyepatch wolf put this  better than I ever could the kind of  design that we are going to relate to is  as varied as what type of person we are  going to be attracted to design is what  the character looks like and how we take  that information in will inevitably  differ from person to person so this is  my take on Durin's design he's boring  okay let me explain his design is very  simplistic and by the books sporting a  top-heavy muscular frame which isn't bad  in my opinion just nothing to write home  about one aspect that did bother me was  the attire that he wore ignoring the  argument that the pride trooper uniform  is better suited to a comedic role which  it has been used in the same uniform is  worn by nine other characters who  between them haven't eliminated a single  person further diluting its appeal and  removing any sense of occasion and  respect that it has for comparison's  sake let's take a look at my favorite  character design from the entire series  Frieza his cold and sterile design helps  to convey his personality perfectly if  someone was to walk in and look at  Frieza on TV with no information they  would have a decent handle on his  character and what he's about so in  saying that does jaren's design have the  same  at first glance mmm kinda Joran is quiet  and stern and I'm not sure if that is  immediately understood just by looking  at them it could be the case that it  does in fact do that for you however for  me it's serviceable at best one thing I  will say is that I am thankful that  Joran has a strong build for a character  Dragon Ball super has enough skinny  strong characters so it was refreshing  to see someone of Geron stature finally  show up button saying that his design is  nothing special it's serviceable the  next part though might be harder to pull  off Frieza not only works because of his  design but also because of his  personality filled to the brim with  self-importance five seconds with Frieza  and you'll be dying to see him on your  screen again in the same vein cell is  smarmy and smug and Boo is volatile and  savage while I am hoping to get across  here is that a character's personality  is arguably its most important aspect I  mean Vegeta is a short angry looking  troll doll with a receding hairline but  god damn it he's one of the best  personalities in the entire series I  can't imagine anyone else filling that  role and that's one of the issues I have  with jiren from a character's point of  view he has virtually nothing to set  himself apart from the crowd it was  Stern and quiet also yes but he was  introduced in a much shorter arc and had  a very interesting move set the latter  of which being the main reason tons of  people liked him again this is Dragon  Ball not Hemingway  we don't need nuanced ematic  deconstructions of Durin's hubris to  enjoy him but something to make him  stand out from the crowd wouldn't go  amiss either everything from what he  wears to what he says or doesn't say to  how he fights nothing about him is  unique or sensational in my opinion  anyway it worked because he is the only  one that could do what he did  characters like Goku Vegeta pick low or  even yeah gob can never be replaced this  is because they have personalities which  we have come to know and identify with  over the course of the show even a  character like Zuma soo was easy to  identify and understand  Geron is so overly simplistic that  literally anyone else could switch into  that role and  nothing would change as long as they  told us that they were the strongest or  whatever in my opinion he's a shell of a  character there's really nothing there  and before episode 127 you could barely  even say that he was a character but  once episode 127 came he did finally get  some exposition this was the spark that  got people turning their heads and  declaring Geron as a bad character to  break it down simply for the vast  majority of the tournament Geron was  given little to no dialogue no  development and absolutely no backstory  past the fact that he was part of the  Pride troopers and was SuperDuper strong  but when his backstory did eventually  surface with four episodes remaining  people were unsatisfied with how cliche  his past was and this is where I think  people are miss directing their  dissatisfaction just because something  is cliche doesn't make it inherently bad  things become cliché for a reason  specifically when they're used over and  over again and things are used over and  over again when they work the  prototypical hero's journey is in almost  every facet of fiction it's a staple  it's by no means bad I mean this is  Dragon Ball we're hardly expecting  Shakespeare levels of narrative  innovation in short there is nothing  wrong with having a character that lost  everything as a child to an evil force  dedicating the rest of his life to  ensuring he never feels that sense of  helplessness again while developing a  complex along the way in fact I kind of  like it so while I don't believe there  is anything wrong with Doran's backstory  personally I think how it was presented  was by and large the culprit for the  outrage in other words they took way too  long  to tell this otherwise simple story but  I have a theory that might explain some  of this away or at least shed some light  Durin's character I think was designed  early on but for the longest time  decisions were not finalized for what  the characters stood for what its goals  were and where he came from my reasoning  for thinking this his presence in the  promo material for the universe survival  arc  in January of 2017 the first animated  promo material aired for the universe  survival arc in which Geron was seen at  Bell modes side a position topo would be  soon swapped in for when the show  eventually aired this tells me one of  two things either Geron was there by  accident or his rule and backstory was  changed retroactively li leading to the  writers of the show intentionally under  developing him resulting in her first  proper meeting being at the tournament  of power and comparing that to the  mangas interpretation is even more  telling that introduction to cheerin  gives us more insight into his values  and character than 30 episodes of the  tournament of power ever did and that's  the problem  gerunds back story isn't sensational by  any means but it's not bad either the  only real issue I had with Joran was how  long it took them to establish this  information with a backstory revealed  four episodes out from the show's ending  how am i as a viewer expected to form a  connection with the character in any  meaningful way it's a little frustrating  because it clearly could have been  something that was established earlier  on to greater effect but with that said  the question remains is during a bad  character from the moment I was  introduced to Jaron I really didn't like  him and as the tournament progressed it  became more and more obvious to me that  Jaron was a hollow character the void of  anything that would interest me at all I  worried he might even ruin the climax of  the entire arc but I was wrong now in  saying that don't paint me as a jury  lover just yet Geron is not a fantastic  character he's not even a good character  he's fine nothing special and nothing  offensively bad and part of me believes  that this is at least on some level  intentional let me explain in one  sentence what I mean the tournament of  power isn't so much a unique arc as it  is a love letter to Dragon Ball now that  might seem like a load of flowery lip  service but think about it how many  iconic moments have been mimicked or  created thanks to this tournament  whether it be Goku's new transformation  a battle with a fuse character the  return of broli  friezes return from oblivion Master  Roshi's speech to Goku and krillen 18  and 17 working together giant monsters  Vegeta being tenacious which leads him  to releasing a blast reminiscent of a  sacrifice in the Buu saga I could easily  go on and I will Super Saiyan God go on  and Goku's kamehameha together  Piccolo's interaction with calming and  male Krillin 18 working together  Gohan blocking a blast just like piccolo  did for him when he was a child this arc  it was designed from the start to be the  last of Super it's Swan son but when  this tournament began none of us knew  that I like many thought things were  going to continue right after much like  it did when the Future Trunks arc ended  and so I hated Juran because I thought  he was going to be the focus  the reason this arc existed to establish  him as a character but he's not the  focus he never was all this time I was  complaining that he wasn't getting the  development he needed when clearly the  focus has been on reliving Dragon Balls  greatest hits rather than specific new  characters Jaron is nothing special he  is there to facilitate these incredible  moments nothing more than a plot device  and he does that job perfectly fine the  real star of the universe survival arc  is Dragon Balls history and it takes  center stage if you enjoyed this vide